// boids — the flocking trio, and a demonstration that it is three lines and
// one new word.
//
// Separation is `push` with a positive gain. Cohesion is the SAME WORD with
// a negative one — the arithmetic is identical and only the direction
// differs, which is why `push` has no sign guard. Alignment needed the
// neighbourhood to carry velocity, and that is all `align` is.
//
// The two radii are the point. A single `near` gives one ring, and a flock
// wants two: a tight one it will not crowd into and a wide one it belongs
// to. Two `near` lines do it, because a `push` or an `align` reads whichever
// `near` is nearest ABOVE it — the slate's own rule, doing a job nobody
// designed it for.
//
//   row.u3  the tight crowd  (a readout)
//   row.u2  the wide crowd   (a readout — and what a colour can read)

spawn
perish

// Do not crowd: a tight ring, strongly.
near plane.drift.@self.k.near | write row.u3
push plane.drift.@self.k.shove

// But belong: a wide ring, gently, and the same word negated. Drive
// `k.flock` positive from a rill and the flock SCATTERS — one number, and
// no part of this file has to know it moved.
near plane.drift.@self.k.far | write row.u2
push plane.drift.@self.k.flock

// And go the way everyone else is going.
align plane.drift.@self.k.match
